**Current support.** The Alpha HTML builder emits routes for normal module roots with authored runtime work or fragments and requires a homepage at the selected project root for directory builds. The deferred `$entry` design will select entries by explicit purpose and replace the mandatory-homepage rule with command-specific no-page rules. It keeps directory routes and path-derived route names.

### Directory-based routes

The module root's directory determines the HTML output path relative to the
selected project root:

[codeblock, $code("text"):
    @page.moth              -> index.html
    docs/@page.moth         -> docs/index.html
    docs/basics/@module.moth -> docs/basics/index.html
    @home.moth              -> index.html
    @api.moth               -> index.html
]

The root module maps to the project index when its activity selects an entry. A nested
directory maps to a nested index route. The normal module-root filename
(`@page.moth`, `@home.moth`, `@api.moth`) has no effect on the route name.

An ordinary file cannot supply an entry, and `index.moth` has no special meaning.
The entry-purpose migration adds no route override, filename-derived route, page alias or
new redirect. Existing `page_url_style` and `redirect_index_html` policies stay
unchanged.

### Entry selection

Today, a normal module root becomes an HTML artefact when it has root activity:
a non-trivial top-level runtime body, a direct const fragment or a runtime
fragment. A direct named builder contribution is a const fragment, so a
metadata-only root still produces a complete document:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    #[$in title:Welcome]
]

One normal root produces at most one directory-derived route entry. The
explicitly selected synthetic single-file root produces the single-file
artefact when it has the required authored content.

The deferred general `$entry` purpose will make entry selection explicit. It is
not current syntax, and the earlier `$page` proposal is superseded. Neither
directive may be written today.

### Deferred command-specific no-page rules

The `$entry` design keeps the current command distinction but changes selection
policy once it lands:

- Directory-project `check` will accept a valid project with no entries.
- HTML application `build` and `dev` will require at least one entry anywhere
  below the selected project root, not necessarily a homepage.
- Single-file HTML output will require an explicit entry purpose on the
  explicitly selected root.

Until that cutover, activity-based selection and the directory-build homepage
requirement remain in force. Do not fabricate `index.html` to satisfy
development tooling. Serve descendant routes normally. If a tool needs a
launch page, choose the real homepage when present or the lexicographically
first normalised page route. That choice doesn't create a redirect or route
alias. Requests to an absent site root retain ordinary missing-route behaviour.

### Builder ownership

The HTML builder owns route and artefact policy. The frontend remains
builder-neutral. The builder decides:

- which active roots produce HTML artefacts
- how routes are derived
- how output files are organised
- whether to reject unsupported reachable features

Source stays target agnostic. The HTML builder may choose JavaScript, Wasm or
mixed output, but source cannot query or branch on that choice. Builder
packages expose stable capabilities rather than target identity.

### Output folders

The builder generates output folders and manages them through manifests.
Builder settings in `config.moth` own output paths. HTML defaults to `_dev` for
development and `_release` for release builds. Every directory output root must
be hidden from source discovery. Treat generated folders as build artefacts,
not source.

### Release versus development output

The default build writes to the hidden dev folder. The `--release` flag writes
to the hidden release output folder. The dev server serves from the dev folder.

### Experimental Wasm mode

In the experimental `--html-wasm` mode, the builder can emit a route folder
containing `index.html`, `page.js` and `page.wasm` instead of a single HTML
file with embedded JavaScript.

### Concepts related to HTML routing and artefacts

- For module identity and cosmetic root suffixes see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For active-root runtime, fragments and dependency non-activation see @#entry-runtime-and-fragments (Active-root runtime and fragments).
- For builder slots and runtime title status see @#entry-config (Entry metadata and builder slots).
